WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.792
The trip to the secret garden had to be postponed for a week.

2
00:00:06.793 --> 00:00:09.589
First the weather was windy.

3
00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:13.305
Then Colin seemed to be coming down with a cold.

4
00:00:13.306 --> 00:00:18.461
In the old days, these two events would have thrown him into a rage.

5
00:00:18.462 --> 00:00:23.978
But Mary visited him every day, and Dickon also came in a few times.

6
00:00:23.979 --> 00:00:29.690
Dickon described what was happening on the moor and in the hedges and on the riverbanks.

7
00:00:29.691 --> 00:00:37.472
Colin almost trembled with excitement when Dickon told him about otter houses and bird nests and rabbit burrows.

8
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:44.486
"The animals are really just like us," Dickon said, "except they have to build their houses every year."

9
00:00:44.487 --> 00:00:51.479
As each day passed, Colin seemed to grow more excited about the mystery surrounding the secret garden.

10
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:56.461
The children did not want anyone to see them when they finally took him there.

11
00:00:56.462 --> 00:01:00.834
They had long talks, planning which paths they would take.

12
00:01:00.835 --> 00:01:07.432
They wanted everyone to think that they were just looking at the new flowers.

13
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:11.410
One day Colin summoned the head gardener to his room.

14
00:01:11.411 --> 00:01:15.016
Mr. Roach was startled when he opened the door.

15
00:01:15.017 --> 00:01:19.138
A crow announced his arrival with a loud caw

16
00:01:19.139 --> 00:01:24.292
! Colin was sitting up in a chair, watching Dickon feed a lamb with a

17
00:01:24.293 --> 00:01:30.413
bottle. "I sent for you," said Colin, "to give you some very important

18
00:01:30.414 --> 00:01:36.124
orders." "Very good, sir." Mr. Roach smiled to himself at Colin's royal

19
00:01:36.125 --> 00:01:41.072
manners. "I am going out in my wheelchair this afternoon," Colin went

20
00:01:41.073 --> 00:01:45.460
on. "If the fresh air agrees with me, I may go out every

21
00:01:45.461 --> 00:01:52.366
day. When I do, none of the gardeners is to be anywhere near the long path by the walled

22
00:01:52.367 --> 00:02:00.079
gardens. I will go out about two o'clock, and everyone must keep away until I send word that they may go back to

23
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.800
work." "Very good, sir," Mr. Roach said

24
00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:06.435
again. Colin turned to

25
00:02:06.436 --> 00:02:10.338
Mary. "What is that thing you say in India when you have finished

26
00:02:10.339 --> 00:02:14.884
talking?" "You say, 'You have my permission to

27
00:02:14.885 --> 00:02:18.420
go.'" Colin, the rajah, waved his

28
00:02:18.421 --> 00:02:23.815
hand. "You have my permission to go," he said to Mr.

29
00:02:23.816 --> 00:02:28.784
Roach. That afternoon the strongest footman carried Colin

30
00:02:28.785 --> 00:02:35.158
downstairs. The servant put Colin in his wheelchair and fixed his cushions and blankets around

31
00:02:35.159 --> 00:02:39.495
him. Dickon began to push the wheelchair slowly and

32
00:02:39.496 --> 00:02:46.387
steadily. Mary walked beside it. Colin leaned back and lifted his face to the

33
00:02:46.388 --> 00:02:51.908
sky. "There are so many sounds of singing and humming," he said.

34
00:02:51.909 --> 00:02:56.925
"What is that sweet scent I smell on every puff of air?"

35
00:02:56.926 --> 00:03:00.964
"It's the flowers on the moor," said Dickon.

36
00:03:00.965 --> 00:03:06.456
It turned out that the children didn't have to worry about seeing anyone.

37
00:03:06.457 --> 00:03:11.561
Not a single human creature was to be found anywhere near the gardens.

38
00:03:11.562 --> 00:03:16.949
But they followed their carefully planned route anyway, just for the fun of it.

39
00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:22.654
They wound in and out among the shrubbery and around the flower beds.

40
00:03:22.655 --> 00:03:29.149
When they finally reached the long path by the walled gardens, they began to speak in whispers.

41
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:35.596
"This is where I used to walk up and down and wonder about the secret garden," said Mary.

42
00:03:35.597 --> 00:03:41.147
"Is it?" Colin began to search the ivy for signs of the door.

43
00:03:41.148 --> 00:03:45.804
"But I don't see anything. There is no door."

44
00:03:45.805 --> 00:03:49.493
"That's what I thought," said Mary.

45
00:03:49.494 --> 00:03:52.630
They walked on in silence.

46
00:03:52.631 --> 00:03:57.574
"This is where the robin flew over the wall," Mary whispered.

47
00:03:57.575 --> 00:04:00.323
"Oh, I wish he'd come again!"

48
00:04:00.324 --> 00:04:08.783
"And that," said Mary, pointing under a bush, "is where he perched on the heap of dirt and showed me the key."

49
00:04:08.784 --> 00:04:15.028
Colin suddenly sat up. "Where? There?" He pointed.

50
00:04:15.029 --> 00:04:17.691
Dickon stopped the wheelchair.

51
00:04:17.692 --> 00:04:24.840
"And this," said Mary, "is where I went to talk to him, and where the wind blew aside the curtain of ivy.

52
00:04:24.841 --> 00:04:29.669
And here is the door. Dickon, push him in quickly!"

53
00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:33.430
Dickon did just that with one strong push.

54
00:04:33.431 --> 00:04:36.620
Colin leaned back against his cushions.

55
00:04:36.621 --> 00:04:42.004
He covered his eyes with his hands until they were inside and the door shut behind them.

56
00:04:42.005 --> 00:04:44.875
Then he dropped his hands.

57
00:04:44.876 --> 00:04:50.931
He looked around the garden just as Mary and Dickon had done the first time they were inside.

58
00:04:50.932 --> 00:04:56.009
A green veil of little leaves had crept over the walls and trees.

59
00:04:56.010 --> 00:05:01.302
Everywhere on the ground there were touches of gold and purple and white.

60
00:05:01.303 --> 00:05:06.519
The trees were showing their pink and white blossoms above Colin's head.

61
00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:12.030
He could hear the fluttering of wings and the singing of birds and the humming of bees.

62
00:05:12.031 --> 00:05:15.548
The sun felt warm on his face.

63
00:05:15.549 --> 00:05:19.170
Mary and Dickon stood and stared at Colin.

64
00:05:19.171 --> 00:05:25.886
He looked so different because a pink glow of color had crept all over his pale face and hands.

65
00:05:25.887 --> 00:05:29.385
"I will get well!" Colin cried.

66
00:05:29.386 --> 00:05:40.033
"Mary! Dickon! I will get well! And I will live forever and ever and ever!"

